The same counter in each framework, showing that the syntax gap is smaller than the reactivity-model gap:

```jsx
// React
function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  return <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>{count}</button>;
}
```

```vue
<!-- Vue -->
<script setup>
import { ref } from 'vue';
const count = ref(0);
</script>
<template>
  <button @click="count++">{{ count }}</button>
</template>
```

```svelte
<!-- Svelte -->
<script>
  let count = 0;
</script>
<button on:click={() => count++}>{count}</button>
```

All three describe the same behavior in roughly the same number of lines. What differs, invisibly, is what happens on click: React re-runs the whole `Counter` function and diffs the result; Vue's `ref` notifies its tracked subscriber (the text binding) directly; Svelte's compiler already generated code that updates exactly that one text node — no component function re-runs at all in either of the latter two.
